1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:08,454
Meet Bob. One personal agent, with one continuing conversation. Let's follow a simple
shopping decision across your phone, two stores, and your desktop.

2
00:00:09,719 --> 00:00:20,131
Start on your phone. Ask for comfortable work sneakers, under one hundred and twenty
dollars, in size nine. Bob saves your brief and finds two candidates from your
connected stores.

3
00:00:28,721 --> 00:00:39,133
Open Dayform. Bob knows which pair you're viewing, and remembers what matters to you.
Ask about comfort for your commute. The answer combines the store's product facts
with your preferences.

4
00:00:45,724 --> 00:00:56,361
Switch to Stride and ask about the first pair. Bob connects the new selection to the
earlier candidate. Day One is the comfort pick. Arc costs more, and exceeds your
budget after tax.

5
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:15,363
Return to Dayform and ask Bob to order. The store prepares a checkout for one hundred
and seven dollars and eighty cents, including demo tax. Nothing is ordered until you
approve.

6
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:33,634
The confirmed receipt is already on your phone. This is a separate interface
connected to the same session. You never need to repeat the decision.

7
00:01:36,962 --> 00:01:47,749
Open the desktop workspace and continue the conversation. Bob still has your budget,
size, and saved candidates. The same interface also runs in the demo's Electron
desktop app.

8
00:01:49,965 --> 00:01:59,858
You control the connections. Budget sharing is optional, and you can revoke a store's
access. Your conversation belongs to your personal session, rather than to any one
store.

9
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:11,967
One conversation. Context from each app. Control stays with you. This is OneAgent,
everywhere. A working demo, with fictional stores and simulated purchases.
